
Batk

- **Simon “Micz Bán”** (fl. 13th c.) – Lord from gens Baksa; _ispán_ (county head) and legendary patriarch.
    
    - **Tamás (I)** de Csapi (fl. *c.*1280–1284) – Son of Simon Micz Bán; the earliest documented Csapi ancestor. In 1284, Tamás and his brothers received a royal grant of the estate **Rát** in Ung County as reward for their service (notably for fighting the Cumans). This established the family’s holdings in the northeast Carpathian region.
        
        - **László (I)** de Csapi (fl. *c.*1312–1320) – Son of Tamás. Served as _főispán_ (county lord) of **Zemplén County**, indicating the family’s rising influence. He married the daughter of a nobleman (Bodon ispán). By the early 14th century the Csapi were among the leading gentry in Upper Hungary. László’s children included:
            
            - **Tamás (II)** “**Dancs**” de Csapi – Son of László I. Also referred to as _Magister Donch_. He succeeded his father as _ispán_ of Zemplén (and appears as “comes de Patak, Zemplén, Liptó” in some records). He married **Klára Tárkői**, a noblewoman. (Some 19th-c. scholars debated his placement in the lineage, but it is clear he was a principal figure of this generation.)
                
            - **Miklós (Nicholas)** de Csapi (fl. 1349) – Son of László I. Served as _ispán_ of **Sáros County**(Sáros vármegye). Miklós is noted in 1349 as _főispán_ of Sáros, demonstrating the family’s continued prominence.
                
            - **János (John)** de Csapi – Son of László I. Mentioned in records (c.1359). His line is less documented; he may have died young or without sons.
                
            - **Mihály (Michael)** de Csapi – Son of László I. He became the forefather of the **Szerdahelyi**noble family, branching off from the Csapi line. (Many Baksa-clan descendants adopted new surnames from their estates.)
                
        - _(Alternate branch):_ **(Simon II de Csapi)** – (Possibly a brother of Tamás I or László I, per some traditions.) This Simon is sometimes credited with founding a Csapi line, but modern genealogy generally folds this into the Tamás line. Notably, a **Simon of the Baksa clan** built a Pauline monastery at Eszeny in 1358 – this could have been a Csapi family member given their local lordship.
            
        - **Early 15th-century descendants:** By this time the family split into two branches named for their estates – **Eszenyi Csapy** and **Polyánkai Csapy** (from _Eszeny_ and _Polyánka_ villages, in Ung/Ungvár region). A prominent figure was:
            
            - **András (Andrew) Csapy** – A grandson or great-grandson of Tamás I (sources differ on exact placement). He is recorded as a royal courtier and in **1418** received a **grant of arms** from King Sigismund. At the Council of Constance, Sigismund bestowed a joint coat-of-arms upon _András Csapy_ along with members of the related Bocskai, Soós, Szerdahelyi, Szürthei, and Széchy families. This honor – which included insignia of the **Order of the Dragon** – was a reward for their loyalty and military service. (It formally acknowledged their common ancestry and noble status.) András’s coat-of-arms became the basis for the family’s heraldry.